WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.994
- Can you name this artist?

00:00:01.994 --> 00:00:05.244
(catchy hip-hop music)

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:10.230
Sounds like Jay-Z covering
Nicki Minaj's "Chun-Li", right?

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.830
Or is Nicki Minaj just Jay-Z sped up?

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.250
This conspiracy has been floating around

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.033
for years and it's hilarious.

00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:20.283
(catchy hip-hop music)

00:00:21.675 --> 00:00:22.520
- What you're actually hearing

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.150
is the effect of pitch shifting,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.340
the result of warping notes,
voices, and other sounds

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.700
to make them sound much
lower or higher than normal.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.170
It's a common tool found in guitar solos

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.070
and hip-hop samples, even cartoons.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.949
But how does it work and
why do people use it?

00:00:38.949 --> 00:00:42.100
(dreamy pop music)

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.880
We're here with Dianna from Physics Girl.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.895
(high pitch sound)
(glass breaks)

00:00:46.895 --> 00:00:48.040
(gasping)

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.330
- My head is not (screaming)

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:53.680
- [Nahre] We were talking
earlier about what

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.580
musicians think of pitches,
as is basically like--

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.960
- A high note is a high
pitch, low note is low pitch.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.650
So in science, we talk about those notes

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.620
as low and high notes, as frequencies.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.453
So check this out.

00:01:05.453 --> 00:01:07.960
If I sort of slide up,
I'm not a great whistler,

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.550
but if I start to slide up the scale,

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.825
like from low pitch to high pitch.

00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:15.075
(high-pitch frequency)

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:18.360
What did you notice?

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.485
- The recording just--
- Yeah!

00:01:19.485 --> 00:01:21.400
- They shrink, come closer.
- They got closer together.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.930
So using a wavelength, which is related

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:25.423
to what we call frequency,

00:01:26.929 --> 00:01:28.610
shorter wavelength is higher frequency.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:29.920
It just happens more often.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.610
- Is that what's happening
when we're pitch shifting?

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.870
We're just taking that and
scrunching the wavelengths?

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.400
- Yes, you would be just
taking your wave form

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:44.260
and squeezing it, making
those peaks closer together.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.510
Or further apart if you pitch shift down.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.130
- Before you hear our original song,

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:49.640
let's talk about how other artists

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.797
have used pitch shifting in their music.

00:01:51.797 --> 00:01:54.160
("Chun-Li" by Nicki Minaj)

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.300
- When we made Nicki
Minaj sound like Jay-Z,

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.720
we slowed down the song to 130%,

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.670
which stretched out the
waves to lower the pitch.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.990
Many artists have used
speed-altering techniques

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.080
to manipulate pitch in their songs,

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.180
but the band that really
pioneered this style

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.636
is one I'm sure you know.

00:02:09.636 --> 00:02:14.219
("Bad Day" by Alvin and the Chipmunks)

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.090
Songwriter, Ross Bagdasarian,

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.070
was experimenting with the tape recorder

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.810
in the late 1950s and discovered a sound

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:24.880
that would define his career.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.350
He used this technique to
create a duet with himself

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.987
in his hit song, "Witch Doctor" from 1958.

00:02:30.987 --> 00:02:34.370
("Witch Doctor" by Ross Bagdasarian Sr.)

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:37.370
Then one day while driving
in Yosemite National Park,

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.310
a chipmunk dashed in front of his car

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.249
and well it all clicked.

00:02:41.249 --> 00:02:42.082
(popping sounds)

00:02:42.082 --> 00:02:42.960
He recorded the vocals

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.860
for Alvin and the Chipmunk
at half the normal speed

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.930
and played the tape back at full speed

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.630
to create the effect.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:50.960
Here's how they sound when you slow

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.501
a chipmunk song back down
to it's original speed.

00:02:54.501 --> 00:02:55.334
(slow a cappella music)

00:02:55.334 --> 00:03:00.334
♪ Where is the moment
we needed the most ? ♪

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:01.700
This technique wasn't new.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.070
For example, it was used in
1939 to give the Munchkins

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.755
of the Wizard of Oz their unique sound.

00:03:07.755 --> 00:03:08.588
- Follow the yellow brick road.

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:10.720
- Follow the yellow brick road.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.947
- It was also used for cartoon
characters like Tweety Bird.

00:03:13.947 --> 00:03:16.280
- I thought I saw a pretty cat.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.440
- Pitch shifting wasn't only used

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.600
for cartoons and magical kingdoms,

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.300
Chuck Berry didn't want
to sound like a chipmunk,

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.480
but his record label did
speed up his recording

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.610
of "Sweet Little Sixteen"
just a little bit,

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.111
so that he sounded younger.

00:03:27.111 --> 00:03:31.170
("Sweet Little Sixteen" by Chuck Berry)

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.650
When creating The Beatles's
"Strawberry Fields Forever,"

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.950
John Lennon wanted to use parts

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.110
of take seven and 26,

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.910
but due to so much
experimentation in the studio,

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.210
the two parts were recorded

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.540
at different tempos and in different keys.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:45.720
Producer George Martin
slowed one tape down

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:46.930
so that the two matched.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.690
You can actually hear
where the splice happens.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:49.850
Listen to the shift right before

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:51.834
John Lennon says, "Going to."

00:03:51.834 --> 00:03:55.470
("Strawberry Fields
Forever" by The Beatles)

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:56.620
You might've noticed John Lennon's

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.380
vocal tape was slowed down,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.110
which is why it sounds
dreamier, almost slurred.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.310
In more recent years,

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.330
producers like Kanye and Just Blaze

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.050
took notes from Ross Bagdasarian

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.540
and sped up soul and R&amp;B samples,

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.080
adding a new Alvin and the Chipmunks

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.259
kind of flavor to their beats.

00:04:12.259 --> 00:04:15.430
("Through the Wire" by Kanye West)

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:16.830
- There's another way to affect pitch

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:19.210
without adjusting the timing at all.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.660
Artists like Jimi
Hendrix use guitar pedals

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.450
to digitally affect
the pitch in real time.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.940
In his song "Purple Haze",
he played the guitar

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.160
through a pedal called the Octavia,

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.670
which electronically doubles

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.990
the frequency of the notes
that are being played,

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:35.423
so that they sound an octave higher.

00:04:35.423 --> 00:04:38.336
(high-pitch guitar riff)

00:04:38.336 --> 00:04:40.670
In more recent years, Jack
White used the guitar pedal

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:44.070
called the Digitech Whammy
to do the exact opposite.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.560
Because The White Stripes
lacked a bass guitarist,

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.400
Jack White pitched down his guitar

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.009
to sound like a bass in the
song "Seven Nation Army".

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:54.950
(bass guitar riff)

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:58.130
Artist have also used pitch
shifting to fix mistakes.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:02.040
Auto-Tune was invented in
1997 to hide studio errors.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.010
Notes that were sung a bit off

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.550
were no longer going through a take.

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:08.620
Producers were able to shift frequencies

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:12.560
of notes to make everything
sound perfectly in tune.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.693
- But then on October
19th, 1998, this happened.

00:05:16.693 --> 00:05:19.693
("Believe" by Cher)

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.230
Auto-Tune evolved from the audio version

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.890
of white out into an instrument itself.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.090
Cher didn't use it to
cover up her mistakes.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:30.170
Instead, she used it as a vocal effect.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:33.150
Some purist still deride it
as a crutch or a gimmick,

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.450
while others like Radiohead,
Lil' Wayne, and T-Paine

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.880
saw it as another tool
to make something unique.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.580
- When singer, Casey Dienel, claimed that

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.920
Justin Bieber and Skrillex's song "Sorry"

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.000
infringed on the copyright
of her song "Ring The Bell,"

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.200
Skrillex took to Twitter

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.960
to show that he didn't steal the song,

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.610
but actually used pitch shifting

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.560
to create the song's chorus in a way

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.580
that made it a very close match.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.980
- [Skrillex] Now take this
♪ Ooooooh ♪

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.567
Pitch it up 12. See my talents.

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:01.460
♪ Ooooooh ♪

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.410
- In recent years, there has been a wave

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.190
of choruses that feature vocals

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.610
with raised pitches and fragmented parts

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:09.830
with producers contorting the human voice

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:12.970
into catchy but alien-sounding hooks.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.500
Think Major Lazer's "Lean On"

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.500
(catchy pop music)

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.225
or Skrillex, Diplo, and
Bieber's "Where Are U Now."

00:06:22.225 --> 00:06:24.020
(catchy pop music)

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:28.020
- What do you think we can
do with the pitch shift song?

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.763
- Maybe taking a sample
of someone talking,

00:06:31.843 --> 00:06:33.140
because there are pitches

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.530
that if you really examine it.
- Yeah.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:39.550
- What if every element of the song

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:41.590
is pitch shifted in song way?

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:44.320
- I feel like it's gonna
sound really trippy

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.830
because everything, every part of,

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:48.790
there's nothing familiar in there anymore

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:50.740
because everything has been altered.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.595
- Trippy is good and is instant.

00:06:52.595 --> 00:06:54.440
Trippy is great. (laughing)

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:55.910
- I hope it sounds trippy.

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.530
- I have the clip of
Dianna speaking right now.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.167
- Push it.
- Let me play it.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.880
- [Dianna] I'm Dianna from Physics Girl

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.600
and you're watching Sound Field.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.900
- We should use her definition of pitch.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.680
We can start the track just to give

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.420
the clips of context but then
gradually pitch shift it.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:16.000
- Alright, so.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.800
- [Dianna] Pitch is the
frequency of the sound wave,

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.740
which tells you how
often your wave is moving

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.880
back and forth. Your wave
is moving back and forth.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.674
- Your wave is moving back
and forth, back and forth.

00:07:26.674 --> 00:07:27.705
(laughing)

00:07:27.705 --> 00:07:30.197
- Back and forth, back and
forth, b-back and forth.

00:07:30.197 --> 00:07:32.261
Back and forth. Back and forth.

00:07:32.261 --> 00:07:34.340
(table hitting)
- Okay.

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:37.578
- We have our track. (laughs)

00:07:37.578 --> 00:07:38.411
(electric synth music)

00:07:38.411 --> 00:07:39.244
- [Dianna] Pitch is the
frequency of the sound wave,

00:07:39.244 --> 00:07:40.077
which tells you how
often your wave is moving

00:07:40.077 --> 00:07:40.910
back and forth. Wave is
moving back and forth.

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:44.160
(electric synth music)

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.460
- As for Jay-Z and Nicki Minaj,

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:16.930
well Jay is the one that wrote,

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:20.060
Death of Auto-Tune, to protest
all this pitch shifting.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:23.410
But then again, if he's
Nicki's ghost rapper,

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:26.114
wouldn't that be the right
move to throw us all off?

00:08:26.114 --> 00:08:28.225
